REST APIs serve as the core interface of modern distributed systems, where operations are highly interconnected and depend on one another. Many severe vulnerabilities arise from hidden dependencies across multiple API operations, making accurate dependency modeling essential for effective testing. Existing approaches rely on basic producer-consumer relationships or simple parameter matching, which introduce false dependencies, cause inefficient exploration, or miss implicit relationships. To address these limitations, we propose a context-aware behavior modeling approach for security testing of RESTful services. Our approach refines dependencies using contextual parameter matching that incorporates path-level and hierarchical semantics prior to exploration, and introduces tentative dependency edges to model and validate uncertain relationships through runtime feedback. Integrated with a multi-agent reinforcement learning-based testing framework, the proposed method generates more relevant and valid and mutated multi-step test sequences, enabling deeper API state exploration and more effective security testing under the same testing budget.